# Line endings are part of correctness here, not style. A CRLF shebang names an interpreter
# called "bash\r" on Linux, and .githooks/pre-commit is a shell script. Pin them regardless of
# the cloning machine's core.autocrlf, so a Windows checkout cannot commit a CRLF hook and a
# POSIX checkout cannot receive one.
# Every tool in this package writes LF unconditionally, and several tests compare generated
# output against the checked-out file byte for byte. With `text=auto` alone a Windows clone
# gets CRLF, the generator writes LF, and the guard that says "a hand-edit cannot be silently
# lost" fails on a fresh clone of a repo that passes in place.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.cjs       text eol=lf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
